<CounterfeitStudy
  title="The 38 Blessings"
  subtitle="Truth Gone Sideways"
  deck="Thirty-eight familiar virtues, and the Root they left behind."
  description="The thirty-eight blessings of the Buddhist Mangala Sutta, set one by one beside Scripture: where each came from in the Word, and where it turned sideways."
>
    <h2>A Book in a Hotel Room</h2>
    <p>In Thai hotel rooms, where an American traveler might expect a Gideon Bible, a small blue book sometimes sits in the drawer instead: <em>The 38 Blessings: The Pillars of Dharma for Prosperity in Life</em>, by Yaowares Bunnag. The gold wheel on its cover is the Dharma wheel of Buddhism, and its eight spokes stand for the Eightfold Path.</p>
    <p>The book is built on a short Buddhist text called the <em>Mangala Sutta</em>, the "Discourse on Blessings." In the story, a heavenly being comes to the Buddha at night and asks what the highest blessing is. The Buddha answers in ten short verses that name thirty-eight blessings, from avoiding fools to a mind at perfect peace. The text sits in the Pali Canon, the Buddhist scriptures, which were first written down in Sri Lanka in the first century BC.</p>
    <p>Much of the list sounds familiar to anyone who reads Scripture. Honor your parents. Avoid fools. Be patient, humble, grateful, and generous. That familiarity is the reason for this study.</p>
    <h2>Why So Much Sounds Familiar</h2>
    <p>Scripture gives the reason. Every nation on earth comes from the sons of Noah (Genesis 10:32), and Yahuah "hath made of one blood all nations of men" (Acts 17:26). Every nation began with the knowledge of the Creator. Paul says they knew Him, yet "glorified him not as God," and traded His glory for images (Romans 1:21-23). What remained was a memory of the truth with the Root cut away.</p>
    <p>Paul adds that even nations without the Law show "the work of the law written in their hearts" (Romans 2:14-15). So when a Buddhist text tells a man to honor his mother, it is not borrowing from Moses. It is repeating what the conscience still knows. No historian has shown that the Buddha drew on the Hebrew Scriptures, and this study does not claim he did.</p>
    <p>A counterfeit works the same way. No one prints a bill that looks like nothing. The closer the copy runs to the real thing, the easier it spends. The study below walks through all thirty-eight blessings in this edition's own wording, grouped into the sutta's ten verses. Each one is set beside the Word: where it came from, and where it turned.</p>
    <h2>The First Verse: The Company a Man Keeps</h2>
    <h3>1. Dissociating from the villain</h3>
    <p><strong>From the Word.</strong> "Blessed is the man that walketh not in the counsel of the ungodly" (Psalm 1:1), and "a companion of fools shall be destroyed" (Proverbs 13:20).</p>
    <p><strong>Sideways.</strong> The sutta measures the fool by Buddhist teaching, not by Yahuah's Law. In Scripture the fool is the one who "hath said in his heart, There is no God" (Psalm 14:1). By that measure, a teaching with no Creator falls on the wrong side of its own first blessing.</p>
    <h3>2. Associating with the wise</h3>
    <p><strong>From the Word.</strong> "He that walketh with wise men shall be wise" (Proverbs 13:20).</p>
    <p><strong>Sideways.</strong> Scripture says where wisdom begins: "The fear of Yahuah is the beginning of wisdom" (Proverbs 9:10). A wise man without the fear of Yahuah is, by that verse, not wise at all.</p>
    <h3>3. Revere the blessed</h3>
    <p><strong>From the Word.</strong> Give "honour to whom honour" is due (Romans 13:7).</p>
    <p><strong>Sideways.</strong> In the sutta, those worthy of honor are the Buddha, his teaching, and his monks, and in Thailand that honor is paid by bowing before images. Bowing to a man as a mark of honor is not wrong in itself. Abraham bowed to the people of the land (Genesis 23:7), and the Greek word <em>proskuneō</em> covers that kind of homage. What goes to Yahuah alone is service: "him only shalt thou serve" (Matthew 4:10). The turn is the image. The Second Commandment forbids bowing down to a carved image or serving it (Exodus 20:4-5).</p>
    <h2>The Second Verse: Place and Footing</h2>
    <h3>4. Inhabiting a suitable milieu</h3>
    <p><strong>From the Word.</strong> Abram was told to leave his country for "a land that I will shew thee" (Genesis 12:1), while Lot chose a well-watered valley that held Sodom (Genesis 13:10-13). Where a man lives matters.</p>
    <p><strong>Sideways.</strong> The suitable place in the sutta is wherever Buddhist teaching is found. In Scripture the suitable place is wherever Yahuah sends, even a tent in a strange land (Hebrews 11:8-10).</p>
    <h3>5. Being commendable</h3>
    <p>This edition titles the fifth blessing "Being commendable." The Pali line behind it, <em>pubbe ca katapuññatā</em>, means "having done merit in the past," and in Buddhist teaching that means merit stored up in former lives.</p>
    <p><strong>From the Word.</strong> Scripture does carry blessing down a line: mercy "unto thousands of them that love me, and keep my commandments" (Exodus 20:6). Isaac was blessed "because that Abraham obeyed my voice" (Genesis 26:5).</p>
    <p><strong>Sideways.</strong> A father's obedience flowing to his children becomes a man's own deeds coming back around from another life. Scripture says "it is appointed unto men once to die, but after this the judgment" (Hebrews 9:27).</p>
    <p class="cfs-deeper"><strong>Go Deeper:</strong> <a href="/counterfeit/buddhism/38-blessings/past-merit">Merit from Past Lives</a></p>
    <h3>6. Maintaining the right position</h3>
    <p><strong>From the Word.</strong> "Ponder the path of thy feet, and let all thy ways be established" (Proverbs 4:26).</p>
    <p><strong>Sideways.</strong> The sutta asks a man to set his own course by his own effort. Jeremiah answers that "it is not in man that walketh to direct his steps" (Jeremiah 10:23). The course is set by the One who gave the Law: "Thy word is a lamp unto my feet" (Psalm 119:105).</p>
    <h2>The Third Verse: Learning, Skill, and Speech</h2>
    <h3>7. Being learned</h3>
    <p><strong>From the Word.</strong> "A wise man will hear, and will increase learning" (Proverbs 1:5).</p>
    <p><strong>Sideways.</strong> The learning in view is learning in the Dharma and in the world. Scripture's learning is the Law, meditated on "day and night" (Psalm 1:2; Joshua 1:8). Paul warns of men "ever learning, and never able to come to the knowledge of the truth" (2 Timothy 3:7).</p>
    <h3>8. Being adroit</h3>
    <p><strong>From the Word.</strong> Bezalel was filled "with the spirit of God, in wisdom... and in all manner of workmanship" (Exodus 31:3), and "a man diligent in his business" will "stand before kings" (Proverbs 22:29).</p>
    <p><strong>Sideways.</strong> This one stays close to the Word. What is missing is the Giver. Bezalel's skill came from Yahuah, not from himself.</p>
    <h3>9. Being self-disciplined</h3>
    <p><strong>From the Word.</strong> "He that ruleth his spirit" is better "than he that taketh a city" (Proverbs 16:32), and self-control is listed among the fruit of the Spirit (Galatians 5:22-23).</p>
    <p><strong>Sideways.</strong> In Scripture, self-control is fruit, grown by the Spirit in one who belongs to Yahuah. In the sutta it is a work a man builds on his own to earn merit.</p>
    <h3>10. Delivering proverbial expression</h3>
    <p><strong>From the Word.</strong> "A word fitly spoken is like apples of gold in pictures of silver" (Proverbs 25:11).</p>
    <p><strong>Sideways.</strong> This one also stays close. Scripture adds the Judge of every word: men will "give account" of "every idle word" in the day of judgment (Matthew 12:36).</p>
    <h2>The Fourth Verse: The Home</h2>
    <h3>11. Caring of parents</h3>
    <p><strong>From the Word.</strong> "Honour thy father and thy mother: that thy days may be long" (Exodus 20:12), "the first commandment with promise" (Ephesians 6:2).</p>
    <p><strong>Sideways.</strong> The sutta keeps the command and drops the Commander. In Thai practice, caring for parents is also a way to make merit, and merit can be dedicated to parents after they die. Scripture knows of no merit sent to the dead, who "know not any thing" (Ecclesiastes 9:5).</p>
    <h3>12. Cherishing one's children</h3>
    <p><strong>From the Word.</strong> "Train up a child in the way he should go" (Proverbs 22:6), and teach Yahuah's words "diligently unto thy children" (Deuteronomy 6:7).</p>
    <p><strong>Sideways.</strong> Deuteronomy 6 puts the commandments at the center of raising a child. Cherishing a child without teaching him Yahuah's Law keeps the affection and loses the inheritance.</p>
    <h3>13. Cherishing one's spouse</h3>
    <p><strong>From the Word.</strong> "Whoso findeth a wife findeth a good thing" (Proverbs 18:22), and "Husbands, love your wives" (Ephesians 5:25).</p>
    <p><strong>Sideways.</strong> Close to the Word here. Yet the same list holds up the celibate monk as the higher path (blessing 32), while Scripture says "It is not good that the man should be alone" (Genesis 2:18).</p>
    <h3>14. Leaving no work undone</h3>
    <p><strong>From the Word.</strong> "Go to the ant, thou sluggard" (Proverbs 6:6), and "Six days shalt thou labour" (Exodus 20:9).</p>
    <p><strong>Sideways.</strong> The commandment that says to labor six days goes on to say the seventh is the Sabbath of Yahuah (Exodus 20:10). The sutta keeps the work and has no Sabbath.</p>
    <h2>The Fifth Verse: Giving and Conduct</h2>
    <h3>15. Love of giving</h3>
    <p><strong>From the Word.</strong> "He that hath pity upon the poor lendeth unto Yahuah" (Proverbs 19:17).</p>
    <p><strong>Sideways.</strong> In Thai practice the chief giving is to monks, and its aim is to make merit. Yahushua taught giving in secret, not for a reward from men (Matthew 6:1-4), and Scripture never makes giving the price of a better next life.</p>
    <p class="cfs-deeper"><strong>Go Deeper:</strong> <a href="/counterfeit/buddhism/38-blessings/merit-for-sale">Merit for Sale</a></p>
    <h3>16. Righteous conduct</h3>
    <p><strong>From the Word.</strong> "Do justly, and... love mercy, and... walk humbly with thy God" (Micah 6:8).</p>
    <p><strong>Sideways.</strong> The standard of righteousness in the sutta is the Dharma. In Scripture the standard is Yahuah's Law: "all thy commandments are righteousness" (Psalm 119:172).</p>
    <h3>17. Caring for Kin</h3>
    <p><strong>From the Word.</strong> A man who does not "provide... for his own" has denied the faith (1 Timothy 5:8), and Torah gave every family a kinsman-redeemer (Leviticus 25:25; Ruth 4).</p>
    <p><strong>Sideways.</strong> Close to the Word. The kinsman-redeemer, though, points past care for relatives to Yahuah Himself, who calls Himself "your redeemer" (Isaiah 43:14).</p>
    <h3>18. Faultless works</h3>
    <p><strong>From the Word.</strong> "Provide things honest in the sight of all men" (Romans 12:17).</p>
    <p><strong>Sideways.</strong> Scripture says no man's works start out faultless: "there is none that doeth good, no, not one" (Romans 3:12). Good works follow after a man is cleansed (Ephesians 2:10); they do not cleanse him.</p>
    <h2>The Sixth Verse: Restraint</h2>
    <h3>19. Avoiding all evils</h3>
    <p><strong>From the Word.</strong> "Abstain from all appearance of evil" (1 Thessalonians 5:22).</p>
    <p><strong>Sideways.</strong> Evil in the sutta is whatever harms and brings bad karma. In Scripture, evil is defined by Yahuah: "sin is the transgression of the law" (1 John 3:4). Paul says he "had not known sin, but by the law" (Romans 7:7).</p>
    <h3>20. Abstinence from intoxicants</h3>
    <p><strong>From the Word.</strong> "Wine is a mocker, strong drink is raging" (Proverbs 20:1), and "be not drunk with wine" (Ephesians 5:18).</p>
    <p><strong>Sideways.</strong> Scripture forbids drunkenness, not wine itself. Wine was poured out at the altar (Numbers 15:5), and Yahushua made it at Cana (John 2:9-10). The book's penalty for drinking, rebirth "into the underworld as demons," has no root in Scripture at all.</p>
    <h3>21. Frequenting Dharma</h3>
    <p><strong>From the Word.</strong> The blessed man delights in Yahuah's Law, "and in his law doth he meditate day and night" (Psalm 1:2).</p>
    <p><strong>Sideways.</strong> The habit is right; the book is wrong. A man can be faithful every day to the wrong teaching.</p>
    <h2>The Seventh Verse: Character</h2>
    <h3>22. Showing respect</h3>
    <p><strong>From the Word.</strong> "Honour all men. Love the brotherhood. Fear God. Honour the king" (1 Peter 2:17).</p>
    <p><strong>Sideways.</strong> The book ties respect for the throne to merit, calling a response to "His Majesty's wish" "an opportunity to gain merit." Peter puts the fear of God before the honor of the king and never mixes the two.</p>
    <h3>23. Being humble</h3>
    <p><strong>From the Word.</strong> "By humility and the fear of Yahuah are riches, and honour, and life" (Proverbs 22:4).</p>
    <p><strong>Sideways.</strong> Scripture's humility is humility before Yahuah. Humility before no one is only good manners.</p>
    <h3>24. Being contented</h3>
    <p><strong>From the Word.</strong> Paul learned "in whatsoever state I am, therewith to be content" (Philippians 4:11), and "godliness with contentment is great gain" (1 Timothy 6:6).</p>
    <p><strong>Sideways.</strong> Paul's contentment rested on the One who strengthened him (Philippians 4:13). The sutta's rests on wanting less. One is trust; the other is the slow shutting down of desire, the road toward Nirvana.</p>
    <h3>25. Being grateful</h3>
    <p><strong>From the Word.</strong> "In every thing give thanks" (1 Thessalonians 5:18).</p>
    <p><strong>Sideways.</strong> Gratitude needs someone to thank. A teaching with no Creator can feel grateful but has no one to thank for the rain and the fruitful seasons (Acts 14:17).</p>
    <h3>26. Listening to the Dharma whenever possible</h3>
    <p><strong>From the Word.</strong> Belief comes "by hearing, and hearing by the word of God" (Romans 10:17).</p>
    <p><strong>Sideways.</strong> What is heard matters as much as the hearing. Yahushua said, "Take heed what ye hear" (Mark 4:24).</p>
    <h2>The Eighth Verse: Teachability</h2>
    <h3>27. Being patient</h3>
    <p><strong>From the Word.</strong> "Let patience have her perfect work" (James 1:4).</p>
    <p><strong>Sideways.</strong> Scripture's patience waits for something: "Be patient therefore, brethren, unto the coming" (James 5:7). Buddhist patience waits for nothing. It only endures.</p>
    <h3>28. Being amenable to admonition</h3>
    <p><strong>From the Word.</strong> "Rebuke a wise man, and he will love thee" (Proverbs 9:8).</p>
    <p><strong>Sideways.</strong> Close to the Word. The question is whose correction. Scripture's correction comes from the Word itself, which is "profitable for doctrine, for reproof, for correction" (2 Timothy 3:16).</p>
    <h3>29. Recognizing the ascetic</h3>
    <p><strong>From the Word.</strong> Israel sought out "a man of God" and "the seer" for counsel (1 Samuel 9:6-9).</p>
    <p><strong>Sideways.</strong> The true prophet was marked by Yahuah's words in his mouth (Deuteronomy 18:18), not by a robe, a shaved head, or a begging bowl.</p>
    <p class="cfs-deeper"><strong>Go Deeper:</strong> <a href="/counterfeit/buddhism/38-blessings/the-ascetic">The Ascetic and the Prophet</a></p>
    <h3>30. Consulting regularly the Dharma</h3>
    <p><strong>From the Word.</strong> "They that feared Yahuah spake often one to another" (Malachi 3:16), and the Bereans "searched the scriptures daily" (Acts 17:11).</p>
    <p><strong>Sideways.</strong> The Bereans checked even an apostle against the Scriptures. The sutta has nothing outside itself to be checked against.</p>
    <h2>The Ninth Verse: The Path</h2>
    <h3>31. Exercising austerities</h3>
    <p><strong>From the Word.</strong> Yahuah did command Israel to "afflict your souls" on Yom Kippur (Leviticus 23:27).</p>
    <p><strong>Sideways.</strong> Yahuah's affliction is one commanded day of turning back to Him. Ascetic austerity is a self-made program of hardship to burn off desire. Paul calls such rules "will worship... and neglecting of the body," of no value against the flesh (Colossians 2:20-23).</p>
    <p class="cfs-deeper"><strong>Go Deeper:</strong> <a href="/counterfeit/buddhism/38-blessings/austerities">Austerities and the Commanded Fast</a></p>
    <h3>32. Practicing chastity</h3>
    <p><strong>From the Word.</strong> Sexual purity is commanded (Exodus 20:14; 1 Thessalonians 4:3-4).</p>
    <p><strong>Sideways.</strong> For a monk, this blessing means lifelong celibacy. Scripture calls marriage honorable (Hebrews 13:4) and names "forbidding to marry" among the doctrines of devils (1 Timothy 4:1-3). Rome's celibate priesthood took the same turn.</p>
    <h3>33. Recognizing the Four Noble Truths</h3>
    <p><strong>From the Word.</strong> "Thy word is truth" (John 17:17), and "thy law is the truth" (Psalm 119:142).</p>
    <p><strong>Sideways.</strong> Four truths found by a man replace the one Truth spoken by Yahuah.</p>
    <p class="cfs-deeper"><strong>Go Deeper:</strong> <a href="/counterfeit/buddhism/38-blessings/four-noble-truths">Four Truths and One Word</a></p>
    <h3>34. Attaining Nirvana</h3>
    <p><strong>From the Word.</strong> Scripture also promises an end to suffering: "God shall wipe away all tears from their eyes" (Revelation 21:4), and "There remaineth therefore a rest" (Hebrews 4:9).</p>
    <p><strong>Sideways.</strong> <em>Nirvana</em> means "blowing out," as a flame is blown out. The hope of Scripture is not to be blown out but raised up (Daniel 12:2; John 5:28-29).</p>
    <p class="cfs-deeper"><strong>Go Deeper:</strong> <a href="/counterfeit/buddhism/38-blessings/nirvana">Blown Out or Raised Up</a></p>
    <h2>The Tenth Verse: The Still Mind</h2>
    <h3>35. Being with unwavering mind</h3>
    <p><strong>From the Word.</strong> "Thou wilt keep him in perfect peace, whose mind is stayed on thee" (Isaiah 26:3).</p>
    <p><strong>Sideways.</strong> Isaiah's mind is unshaken because it is "stayed on thee." The sutta's mind is unshaken because it is stayed on nothing.</p>
    <h3>36. Freeing the mind from sorrow</h3>
    <p><strong>From the Word.</strong> "Blessed are they that mourn: for they shall be comforted" (Matthew 5:4).</p>
    <p><strong>Sideways.</strong> Scripture does not treat sorrow as the enemy. Yahushua wept (John 11:35), and "godly sorrow worketh repentance" (2 Corinthians 7:10). A mind trained out of all sorrow can no longer repent.</p>
    <h3>37. Purifying the mind</h3>
    <p><strong>From the Word.</strong> "Create in me a clean heart, O God" (Psalm 51:10).</p>
    <p><strong>Sideways.</strong> David asked Yahuah to make the clean heart. The sutta asks a man to make it himself, and Scripture answers, "Who can say, I have made my heart clean?" (Proverbs 20:9).</p>
    <h3>38. Allowing bliss to the mind</h3>
    <p><strong>From the Word.</strong> "In thy presence is fulness of joy" (Psalm 16:11).</p>
    <p><strong>Sideways.</strong> The joy of Scripture is found in a Presence. The bliss of the sutta is found in the absence of everything.</p>
    <h2>Merit for Sale</h2>
    <p>The last chapters of the book show what the blessings are for. Keeping the First Precept for a whole day "is a way of merit making," and the book lists the rewards: living happily, waking happily, no bad dreams, being "protected by gods," "fine skin," and "a much longer lifespan with healthy physical condition." Thais are called fortunate to be born into Buddhism, with "great opportunity to make merit for themselves, the nation, the religion, and the King." Breaking the precept against intoxicants brings rebirth "as demons" to "serve their terms in hell."</p>
    <p>The same pattern lives in Christian circles: the prosperity preacher's "sow a seed, reap a harvest," and Rome's treasury of merit, indulgences, and purgatory. Scripture does tie blessing to obedience (Deuteronomy 28:1-14). What it never does is let a man bank merit and spend it on health, wealth, or another man's soul.</p>
    <h2>Fire, Water, and Storm</h2>
    <p>The book closes with "the moment when the world coming to an end." It names three causes: greed, which burns like fire; anger, which brings danger by water; and illusion, which brings storms. In the book, the world ends under the weight of human vice.</p>
    <p>Scripture also tells of a world that perished by water and a world "reserved unto fire" (2 Peter 3:6-7). The difference is who acts. The Flood came because Yahuah saw that "the wickedness of man was great" and said, "I will destroy man" (Genesis 6:5-7). The coming fire is kept for "the day of judgment and perdition of ungodly men" (2 Peter 3:7). The book keeps the fire and the water and removes the Judge.</p>
    <h2>The Pattern</h2>
    <p>Set side by side, the thirty-eight show one pattern. The fruit is kept: honor your parents, avoid the fool, give, be patient, be content. The Root is cut off: no Creator, no Law spoken from Sinai, no Messiah. In place of Yahuah's favor stands merit, earned by a man and banked against his next life. The blessings that sound most like Scripture are the everyday ones near the start. The ones that turn hardest come at the end, where the path leads not to the Father but to the blowing out of the self.</p>
    <p>Revelation names the people who come through every counterfeit still standing: "Here is the patience of the saints: here are they that keep the commandments of God, and the faith of Yahushua" (Revelation 14:12). Patience, the twenty-seventh blessing, finds its true home there, joined to the commandments of Yahuah and belief in His Son.</p>
</CounterfeitStudy>
